MODEL:
This paper introduces the first publicly accessible 3D orthodontic dental dataset containing paired pre- and post-treatment models designed for training and evaluating automatic tooth alignment algorithms.

Paper Summary
* Background & Problem: Traditional orthodontic planning requires iterative, subjective, and time-consuming back-and-forth communication between orthodontists and dental technicians to design target tooth positions. While deep learning methods can automate tooth arrangement, progress has been bottlenecked by the absence of publicly available 3D dental datasets containing paired pre- and post-treatment models.
* Dataset Scale & Source: Sourced from 435 patients treated with clear aligners at Beijing Stomatological Hospital, the dataset contains 1,060 high-quality pre- and post-orthodontic 3D dental model pairs.
* Acquisition & Filtering: Pre-treatment models were captured using iTero intraoral scanners, while post-treatment target models were collaboratively planned by orthodontists and technicians for actual clear aligner fabrication. A panel of experienced orthodontists filtered out models scoring below clinical acceptability criteria (based on Tweed’s Six Keys and ABO scoring standards).
* Rich Annotations:
  * Individual Crown Segmentation: Mesh models in binary closed STL format with individual tooth crowns and gingiva separated, preserving complete interproximal contact surfaces.
  * FDI Tooth Numbering: Standard tooth position numbering mapped from 2D occlusal projections to 3D models.
  * Anatomical Landmarks: High-precision 3D crown landmark annotations provided for 200 representative model pairs.
  * Malocclusion Classifications: Categorized by crowding severity, overbite, overjet, Angle's malocclusion classes, and dentition stage (permanent, deciduous, mixed).
* Technical Validation: The authors benchmarked four deep learning architectures—TANet, PSTN, TAligNet, and TADPM. TADPM (a diffusion probabilistic model) performed best across alignment metrics (such as point-wise mean distance and rotation error). However, evaluating occlusion indicators revealed a noticeable gap between predicted alignments and ground truth, underscoring that current models still struggle with clinical occlusion constraints and collisions.

Why the Dataset Is Useful
* Fills a Critical Data Gap: Prior open-source dental datasets focused heavily on 2D panoramic radiographs, CBCT scans, or single-stage 3D tooth segmentation (such as Teeth3DS). This is the first public repository providing paired 3D pre/post-treatment models specifically for tooth alignment.
* Clinically Viable Ground Truth: The post-treatment models represent approved target outcomes used in real aligner manufacturing, ensuring that models learn valid, clinically sound dental movements rather than synthetic or unrealistic geometry.
* Challenging, Real-World Diversity: Rather than simple cases, it includes complex clinical scenarios, such as 194 moderate-to-severe crowding cases, 352 deep overbites, and 534 deep overjets, fostering robust algorithm generalization.
* High-Fidelity Segmented Geometry: Utilizing iTero intraoral scanning data fills adjacent tooth contact areas that were previously incomplete or missing in older plaster-cast scans, preserving true crown morphology.
* Standardized Benchmark for Future AI Research: By providing a defined train/test split, multiple baseline implementations, and landmark-based occlusion evaluation criteria, it provides a unified benchmark to develop next-generation AI models that integrate biological and occlusal collision constraints.

