how does this paper differ from the previous paper?

MODEL:
While both papers focus on 3D deep learning applications in digital orthodontics, they target fundamentally different stages of the digital pipeline, solve distinct technical problems, and contribute different core deliverables.

| Dimension | Previous Paper (Automatic Tooth Alignment) | This Paper (Feature-Guided Multilayer Encoding–Decoding Network) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Core Objective | Tooth Alignment Planning: Predict target post-treatment tooth positions from pre-treatment models. | Semantic Tooth Segmentation: Decompose a raw 3D intraoral scan into individual labeled teeth and gingiva, specifically under malocclusion. |
| Primary Contribution | A New Benchmark Dataset: Released the first public repository of 1,060 paired pre/post-treatment 3D models with landmark annotations. | A Novel Network Architecture: Introduced a lightweight U-shaped point cloud network with local mean/global std normalization, push–pull density tuning, and inverted bottlenecks. |
| Data Nature | Paired Temporal Data: Matches pre-treatment scans to finalized post-treatment aligner models for the same patient. | Single-Stage Scans: Evaluates single 3D scans of malformed dentitions (half-jaw models, Teeth3DS, and 3D-IOSSeg). |
| Clinical Focus | Treatment Trajectory & Occlusion: Evaluating final bite relationships (overjet, overbite, Tweed’s Six Keys, collisions). | Scan Intake & Anomaly Handling: Resolving confusing boundaries caused by crowding, diastema, "black triangles," missing teeth, or retained roots. |
| Computational Footprint | Benchmarked heavier generative/alignment architectures (e.g., TADPM diffusion model, TANet). | Optimized for extreme efficiency: 1.54M parameters with a 44%–94% reduction in FLOPs compared to standard baselines. |

Key Technical Differences
* Pre-requisite vs. Downstream Task: The model proposed in this paper acts as the essential first step for the previous paper's task. Before an algorithm can simulate tooth movement or alignment (as in the previous paper), a raw intraoral mesh must first be automatically segmented into individual tooth instances with correct semantic labels—which is precisely what this paper achieves.
* Architecture vs. Dataset Scope: The previous paper primarily serves as a benchmark and dataset release to establish a public standard for tooth arrangement algorithms. In contrast, this paper is primarily an architectural contribution designed to fix point-cloud irregularity, feature confusion between adjacent crowded teeth, and high memory footprints in existing segmentation networks.
* Handling Anatomical Irregularity: While the previous dataset filtered cases based on clinical acceptability for aligner therapy, this paper specifically targets severe anatomical disruptions (e.g., erupting small teeth, implant screw roots, gingival recession gaps) that typically cause standard point cloud networks to mislabel adjacent teeth.

