No, you do not need—and should not—download the training datasets into your GitHub repository. 

Datasets are for Training, Not Running
The datasets listed in the paper's data availability section (such as 3D-IOSSeg or Teeth3DS) contain thousands of heavy 3D mesh files. These are used by researchers to teach the AI how to segment teeth. Once the AI learns this, the knowledge is compressed into a small mathematical model (its "weights"), meaning the original dataset is no longer needed to process new scans. 

Repository Limits and Performance
GitHub repositories are designed for source code and lightweight web assets. Uploading gigabytes of 3D training data will violate GitHub's size constraints, bloat your repository, and severely degrade your website's load times. 

What You Actually Need for the Feature
To make the interactive odontogram function in your live environment, you only need one of two things:
* A Cloud Backend (Recommended): A separate server (e.g., Python FastAPI on AWS or Heroku) hosting the trained model. Your index.html simply sends the user's uploaded .stl file to this server, and the server returns the segmentation coordinates.
* A Web-Exported Model: If the 1.54M-parameter model is converted to a web-friendly format like ONNX or TensorFlow.js, it can be hosted as a single, small file (typically a few megabytes) and run directly in the browser. 

